  "chapterSummary": "Control and coordination allow organisms to detect changes and produce organised responses. Nervous coordination uses receptors, neurons, synapses, the spinal cord and brain. Reflex arcs provide rapid protective responses, while the brain integrates information and controls many voluntary and involuntary functions. Plants lack a nervous system but coordinate through hormones and growth responses such as phototropism and geotropism. Endocrine glands release hormones that regulate growth, metabolism, stress responses and internal balance. Nervous and hormonal systems complement one another.",
